# Changing Directories (cd) - Linux

In Linux, directories are folders.  
The `cd` command is used to **change the current working directory**.

---

## Check Current Directory

```bash
pwd


## Absolute Path
An **absolute path** starts from the **root directory `/`** and gives the full location.

Example:
```bash
cd /home/user/Documents


## Relative Path
A **relative path** starts from your **current directory**.


## Shortcuts
| Command | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| `pwd` | show current directory |
| `cd folder` | go into folder |
| `cd ..` | go back one directory |
| `cd /` | go to root directory |
| `cd ~` | go to home directory |
| `cd -` | go to previous directory |
 `./` | current directory (this folder) |
| `->` | points to the target of a symbolic link (symlink) |
